        <div class="head">
          <h2 id="browser-title">200 OK in your API client. Blocked in Chrome.</h2>
          <p>API clients skip the checks a browser makes, so CORS and CSP bugs show up only after the frontend ships. Give Kurlo your page’s origin and it sends the request the way Chrome would, then tells you in one line why the browser would refuse it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="consoles">
          <article class="console">
            <header><span>A typical API client</span><code>no origin</code></header>
            <ol class="console-body">
              <li><span class="get">GET</span> https://api.example.com/me</li>
              <li><span class="ok">200 OK</span> <span class="dim">· 142 ms · 1.2 KB</span></li>
              <li class="dim">{'{ "id": 42, "email": "ada@example.com" }'}</li>
            </ol>
            <footer>Looks fine. Ships on Friday.</footer>
          </article>
          <article class="console console-kurlo">
            <header><span>Kurlo, browser emulation on</span><code>origin https://app.example.com · credentials</code></header>
            <ol class="console-body">
              <li><span class="opt">OPTIONS</span> /me <span class="ok">→ 204</span> <span class="dim">preflight passed</span></li>
              <li><span class="get">GET</span> /me <span class="ok">→ 200</span></li>
              <li class="error">CORS error: the response allows origin *, but a credentialed request needs the exact origin https://app.example.com</li>
            </ol>
            <footer>What your users would see, found before they see it.</footer>
          </article>
        </div>
        <ul class="checks">
          {checks.map(check => (
